@@@ problem c1 reverse-string-in-place
title: Reverse a String In Place
difficulty: Easy
topics: Strings, Pointers, Two Pointers
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=strlen(s)

=== description
Write `void reverse_string(char *s)` that reverses the NUL-terminated string `s` **in place**. Do not allocate memory and do not use library functions other than (optionally) `strlen`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= strlen(s) <= 10^5`
- `s` contains printable ASCII characters

=== hints
- Find the end of the string first (the character before `'\0'`).
- Swap `*left` and `*right`, move the pointers toward each other, stop when they meet.

=== template
/* Reverse the NUL-terminated string s in place. */
void reverse_string(char *s) {
    /* your code here */
}

=== driver
extern "C" void reverse_string(char *s);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>();
        std::vector<char> buf(s.begin(), s.end());
        buf.push_back('\0');
        c.time([&] { reverse_string(buf.data()); });
        if (buf.back() != '\0') { c.out_text("\"string terminator was overwritten\""); return; }
        c.out(std::string(buf.data()));
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.str(n, "abcdefgXYZ 012!")); });
}

=== approach
Use two pointers: `left` at the first character and `right` at the last one (found by walking to the `'\0'`). Swap the characters they point to and move them toward each other until they meet. Every character moves once, and only one temporary `char` is needed.

=== solution
void reverse_string(char *s) {
    if (s == NULL || *s == '\0') return;
    char *left = s;
    char *right = s;
    while (*(right + 1) != '\0') right++;   /* right -> last character */
    while (left < right) {
        char tmp = *left;                   /* swap the two ends */
        *left++ = *right;
        *right-- = tmp;
    }
}

=== examples
"hello"
> "olleh"

"a b"
> "b a"

""
> ""
: The empty string stays empty.

=== tests
"x"
> "x"

"ab"
> "ba"

"racecar"
> "racecar"

"Hello, World!"
> "!dlroW ,olleH"

@@@ problem c2 string-length
title: String Length (my_strlen)
difficulty: Easy
topics: Strings, Pointers
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=strlen(s)

=== description
Implement `size_t my_strlen(const char *s)`, which returns the number of characters before the terminating `'\0'`, without calling `strlen`.

Bonus: try the idiomatic pointer-difference version `end - s`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= length <= 10^6`

=== hints
- Walk a pointer until it points at `'\0'`; the answer is the distance travelled.

=== template
/* Return the length of s, not counting the terminating '\0'. */
size_t my_strlen(const char *s) {
    /* your code here */
    return 0;
}

=== driver
extern "C" size_t my_strlen(const char *s);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>();
        size_t r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = my_strlen(s.c_str()); });
        c.out((long long)r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.str(n, "abc xyz")); });
}

=== approach
Advance a pointer `p` from `s` until `*p == '\0'`. Pointer subtraction `p - s` then gives the number of characters passed, which is the length.

=== solution
size_t my_strlen(const char *s) {
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p != '\0') p++;     /* stop at the terminator */
    return (size_t)(p - s);     /* distance travelled */
}

=== examples
"hello"
> 5

""
> 0

=== tests
"a"
> 1

"hello world"
> 11

"  "
> 2

@@@ problem c3 string-compare
title: String Compare (my_strcmp)
difficulty: Easy
topics: Strings, Pointers
params: a, b
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=common_prefix_length

=== description
Implement `int my_strcmp(const char *a, const char *b)` with the semantics of the standard `strcmp`: return a **negative** value if `a` sorts before `b`, **zero** if they are equal, and a **positive** value otherwise. Characters are compared as `unsigned char`.

The judge prints the sign of your result: `-1`, `0` or `1`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= strlen(a), strlen(b) <= 10^5`
- printable ASCII

=== hints
- Advance both pointers while the characters are equal and not `'\0'`.
- The result is decided by the first differing characters (a shorter string compares as if it had `'\0'` there).

=== template
/* Compare two strings like strcmp: <0, 0 or >0. */
int my_strcmp(const char *a, const char *b) {
    /* your code here */
    return 0;
}

=== driver
extern "C" int my_strcmp(const char *a, const char *b);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string a = c.arg<std::string>(), b = c.arg<std::string>();
        int r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = my_strcmp(a.c_str(), b.c_str()); });
        c.out(r < 0 ? -1 : r > 0 ? 1 : 0);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string a = g.str(n, "ab"), b = a;
        int r = g.irange(0, 3);
        if (n > 40) r = 0;                      // benchmark: equal strings, full scan
        if (r == 1 && !b.empty()) b[g.irange(0, (int)b.size() - 1)] = 'c';
        if (r == 2) b = b.substr(0, g.irange(0, (int)b.size()));
        if (r == 3) b += "a";
        return g.coin() ? lc::lines(a, b) : lc::lines(b, a);
    });
}

=== approach
Walk both strings together while the current characters are equal and not the terminator. Then return the difference of the current characters, **cast to `unsigned char`** first so that characters above 127 compare correctly. If both reached `'\0'` at the same time, the difference is 0.

=== solution
int my_strcmp(const char *a, const char *b) {
    while (*a != '\0' && *a == *b) {   /* skip the common prefix */
        a++;
        b++;
    }
    /* first difference decides; '\0' sorts before any character */
    return (int)(unsigned char)*a - (int)(unsigned char)*b;
}

=== examples
"apple"
"apple"
> 0

"apple"
"apricot"
> -1
: 'p' < 'r' at index 2.

"b"
"a"
> 1

"app"
"apple"
> -1
: A proper prefix sorts first.

=== tests
""
""
> 0

""
"a"
> -1

"Z"
"a"
> -1

"abc"
"ab"
> 1

@@@ problem c4 string-to-integer-atoi
title: String to Integer (my_atoi)
difficulty: Medium
topics: Strings, Parsing, Overflow
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Implement `int my_atoi(const char *s)`, converting a string to a 32-bit signed integer:

1. skip leading spaces (`' '`);
2. read an optional `'+'` or `'-'`;
3. read digits until a non-digit or the end of the string; leading zeros are fine;
4. if no digits were read, the result is `0`;
5. **clamp** out-of-range values to `INT_MIN` / `INT_MAX`.

Avoid undefined behaviour: never let a signed `int` overflow while accumulating.

=== constraints
- `0 <= strlen(s) <= 200`
- `s` contains letters, digits, `' '`, `'+'`, `'-'` and `'.'`

=== hints
- Before `result = result * 10 + digit`, check whether that would exceed `INT_MAX` (compare with `INT_MAX / 10` and `INT_MAX % 10`).
- Or accumulate in a `long long` and stop once it passes `2^31`.

=== template
/* Parse a signed 32-bit integer, clamping on overflow. */
int my_atoi(const char *s) {
    /* your code here */
    return 0;
}

=== driver
extern "C" int my_atoi(const char *s);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>();
        int r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = my_atoi(s.c_str()); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) {
        std::string s(g.irange(0, 3), ' ');
        int kind = g.irange(0, 5);
        if (kind == 0) s += g.str(g.irange(0, 3), "+-");
        else if (g.coin()) s += "+-"[g.irange(0, 1)];
        if (kind == 1) s += std::string(g.irange(1, 4), '0');
        s += g.str(g.irange(0, kind == 2 ? 14 : 8), "0123456789");
        if (g.coin(0.4)) s += g.str(g.irange(1, 4), "ab .-1");
        if (kind == 3) s = "words " + s;
        return lc::lines(s);
    });
}

=== approach
Follow the rules step by step with a pointer: skip spaces, read one optional sign, then consume digits. Keep the magnitude in a `long long` accumulator; as soon as it exceeds `2^31` we know the result is clamped and can stop (this also keeps the accumulator from overflowing on very long digit strings). Finally apply the sign and clamp to `[INT_MIN, INT_MAX]`.

=== solution
int my_atoi(const char *s) {
    while (*s == ' ') s++;                       /* 1) leading spaces */
    int sign = 1;
    if (*s == '+' || *s == '-') {                /* 2) optional sign */
        if (*s == '-') sign = -1;
        s++;
    }
    long long value = 0;
    while (*s >= '0' && *s <= '9') {            /* 3) digits */
        value = value * 10 + (*s - '0');
        if (value > 2147483648LL) break;         /* already out of range: stop early */
        s++;
    }
    value *= sign;
    if (value > INT_MAX) return INT_MAX;         /* 5) clamp */
    if (value < INT_MIN) return INT_MIN;
    return (int)value;
}

=== examples
"42"
> 42

"   -042"
> -42
: Spaces are skipped and leading zeros ignored.

"1337c0d3"
> 1337
: Reading stops at the first non-digit.

"0-1"
> 0

"words and 987"
> 0
: The first non-space character isn't a sign or digit.

=== tests
""
> 0

"-91283472332"
> -2147483648

"91283472332"
> 2147483647

"+-12"
> 0

"-2147483648"
> -2147483648

"2147483647"
> 2147483647

"   +0 123"
> 0

@@@ problem c5 integer-to-string-itoa
title: Integer to String (int_to_str)
difficulty: Medium
topics: Strings, Math, Overflow
params: value
time: O(number of digits)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Implement `char *int_to_str(int value, char *buf)`, which writes the decimal representation of `value` into `buf` (NUL-terminated) and returns `buf`. `buf` has room for at least 12 characters.

Watch out for `INT_MIN`: `-INT_MIN` does not fit in an `int`.

=== constraints
- `INT_MIN <= value <= INT_MAX`

=== hints
- Produce digits with `% 10` and `/ 10`: they come out in reverse order.
- Work with an `unsigned` (or `long long`) magnitude so that `INT_MIN` is not a special case.

=== template
/* Write value in decimal into buf (at least 12 bytes) and return buf. */
char *int_to_str(int value, char *buf) {
    /* your code here */
    return buf;
}

=== driver
extern "C" char *int_to_str(int value, char *buf);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        int v = c.arg<int>();
        char buf[32];
        std::memset(buf, 'X', sizeof buf);
        char *r = nullptr;
        c.time([&] { r = int_to_str(v, buf); });
        if (r != buf) { c.out_text("\"must return buf\""); return; }
        if (!std::memchr(buf, '\0', 12)) { c.out_text("\"missing '\\\\0' within 12 bytes\""); return; }
        c.out(std::string(buf));
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) {
        int k = g.irange(0, 3);
        int v = k == 0 ? g.irange(-9, 9) : k == 1 ? g.irange(-100000, 100000) : g.irange(INT_MIN, INT_MAX);
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
Take the magnitude as an `unsigned int`: for negative values, `0u - (unsigned)value` is well defined and handles `INT_MIN` correctly. Emit digits with `% 10` into a small local buffer (they come out least-significant first), then copy them to `buf` in reverse order after an optional `'-'`, and terminate the string.

=== solution
char *int_to_str(int value, char *buf) {
    unsigned int mag = value < 0 ? 0u - (unsigned int)value : (unsigned int)value;  /* safe for INT_MIN */
    char tmp[12];
    int n = 0;
    do {                                   /* do-while so that 0 prints "0" */
        tmp[n++] = (char)('0' + mag % 10);
        mag /= 10;
    } while (mag > 0);

    char *p = buf;
    if (value < 0) *p++ = '-';
    while (n > 0) *p++ = tmp[--n];         /* digits were produced in reverse */
    *p = '\0';
    return buf;
}

=== examples
123
> "123"

-45
> "-45"

0
> "0"

=== tests
-2147483648
> "-2147483648"

2147483647
> "2147483647"

-1
> "-1"

1000
> "1000"

@@@ problem c6 find-substring-strstr
title: Find a Substring (my_strstr)
difficulty: Medium
topics: Strings, Pointers, String Matching
params: haystack, needle
time: O(n · m)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=strlen(haystack)_(short_needle)

=== description
Implement `int my_strstr(const char *haystack, const char *needle)`: return the index of the first occurrence of `needle` in `haystack`, or `-1` if there is none. An empty `needle` matches at index `0`.

Do not call `strstr`, `strncmp` or `memcmp`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= strlen(haystack), strlen(needle) <= 10^5`

=== hints
- For every start position, compare characters until the needle ends (match) or a character differs.
- You can stop early once fewer characters than `strlen(needle)` remain.

=== template
/* Index of the first occurrence of needle in haystack, or -1. */
int my_strstr(const char *haystack, const char *needle) {
    /* your code here */
    return -1;
}

=== driver
extern "C" int my_strstr(const char *haystack, const char *needle);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string a = c.arg<std::string>(), b = c.arg<std::string>();
        int r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = my_strstr(a.c_str(), b.c_str()); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string hay = g.lower(n, 3);
        std::string nd = g.lower(g.irange(0, 4), 3);
        if (n > 40) nd = "abcabd";                           // benchmark: short needle, not present
        else if (g.coin(0.4) && n > 0) { int p = g.irange(0, n - 1); nd = hay.substr(p, g.irange(1, n - p)); }
        return lc::lines(hay, nd);
    });
}

=== approach
The straightforward scan: for each start position `i`, compare `haystack[i + k]` with `needle[k]` for `k = 0, 1, ...`. If the needle's terminator is reached, `i` is the answer; if the haystack's terminator is reached first, no later start can fit either, so return `-1`. Worst case O(n · m), which is fine for short needles (KMP gives O(n + m)).

=== solution
int my_strstr(const char *haystack, const char *needle) {
    if (*needle == '\0') return 0;                  /* empty needle matches at 0 */
    for (int i = 0; haystack[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        int k = 0;
        while (needle[k] != '\0' && haystack[i + k] == needle[k]) k++;
        if (needle[k] == '\0') return i;            /* whole needle matched */
        if (haystack[i + k] == '\0') return -1;     /* ran out of haystack: no later match fits */
    }
    return -1;
}

=== examples
"hello"
"ll"
> 2

"aaaaa"
"bba"
> -1

"abc"
""
> 0

=== tests
""
""
> 0

""
"a"
> -1

"mississippi"
"issip"
> 4

"abc"
"abcd"
> -1

@@@ problem c7 reverse-words-in-place
title: Reverse the Words of a String In Place
difficulty: Medium
topics: Strings, Pointers, Two Pointers
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=strlen(s)

=== description
Implement `void reverse_words(char *s)`, which reverses the **order of the words** in `s`, in place. Words are separated by **single spaces**, and there are no leading or trailing spaces. The letters inside each word keep their order.

Use O(1) extra memory: no second buffer.

=== constraints
- `0 <= strlen(s) <= 10^5`
- `s` consists of letters, digits and single spaces between words

=== hints
- Reverse the whole string. Now the words are in the right order but each word is backwards.
- Then reverse each word individually.

=== template
/* Reverse the order of the space-separated words of s, in place. */
void reverse_words(char *s) {
    /* your code here */
}

=== driver
extern "C" void reverse_words(char *s);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>();
        std::vector<char> buf(s.begin(), s.end());
        buf.push_back('\0');
        c.time([&] { reverse_words(buf.data()); });
        c.out(std::string(buf.data()));
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string s;
        while ((int)s.size() < n) s += (s.empty() ? "" : " ") + g.str(g.irange(1, 6), "abcdefXYZ019");
        return lc::lines(s);
    });
}

=== approach
Two reversals do the job:

1. reverse the **entire** string: `"the sky is blue"` → `"eulb si yks eht"`. The words are now in reverse order, but each word is spelled backwards;
2. reverse **each word** separately: `"blue is sky the"`.

A helper that reverses the range `[lo, hi]` with two pointers is all you need. Every character is moved twice: O(n) time, O(1) space.

=== solution
static void reverse_range(char *lo, char *hi) {
    while (lo < hi) {
        char t = *lo;
        *lo++ = *hi;
        *hi-- = t;
    }
}

void reverse_words(char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);
    if (n == 0) return;
    reverse_range(s, s + n - 1);              /* 1) reverse everything */
    char *word = s;
    for (char *p = s;; p++) {                 /* 2) fix each word */
        if (*p == ' ' || *p == '\0') {
            reverse_range(word, p - 1);
            if (*p == '\0') break;
            word = p + 1;
        }
    }
}

=== examples
"the sky is blue"
> "blue is sky the"

"hello"
> "hello"

"a b"
> "b a"

=== tests
""
> ""

"x y z"
> "z y x"

"C is fun 101"
> "101 fun is C"

@@@ problem c8 squeeze-characters
title: Squeeze: Delete Characters
difficulty: Medium
topics: Strings, Two Pointers, Lookup Table
params: s, remove
time: O(n + m)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=strlen(s)

=== description
A classic from K&R: implement `void squeeze(char *s, const char *remove)`, which deletes **every** character of `s` that appears anywhere in `remove`, in place. The remaining characters keep their order.

Aim for O(n + m) time: don't scan `remove` again for every character of `s`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= strlen(s) <= 10^5`, `0 <= strlen(remove) <= 128`
- printable ASCII

=== hints
- Build a 256-entry lookup table: `kill[c]` is true if `c` appears in `remove`.
- Then use a read pointer and a write pointer over `s`, and terminate the result with `'\0'`.

=== template
/* Delete from s every character that occurs in remove (in place). */
void squeeze(char *s, const char *remove) {
    /* your code here */
}

=== driver
extern "C" void squeeze(char *s, const char *remove);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>(), rm = c.arg<std::string>();
        std::vector<char> buf(s.begin(), s.end());
        buf.push_back('\0');
        c.time([&] { squeeze(buf.data(), rm.c_str()); });
        c.out(std::string(buf.data()));
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        return lc::lines(g.str(n, "abcdef .!ABC"), g.str(g.irange(0, 5), "aceB. "));
    });
}

=== approach
First mark the characters to delete in a lookup table of 256 flags (indexed by `unsigned char`). Then compact `s` with **two pointers**: `read` visits every character; characters not marked are copied to `write`, which only advances for kept characters. Finally write the terminator at `write`. Each string is scanned once.

=== solution
void squeeze(char *s, const char *remove) {
    bool kill[256] = {false};                       /* characters to delete */
    for (const unsigned char *r = (const unsigned char *)remove; *r; r++) kill[*r] = true;

    char *write = s;
    for (const char *read = s; *read; read++) {
        if (!kill[(unsigned char)*read]) *write++ = *read;   /* keep it */
    }
    *write = '\0';                                  /* new end of string */
}

=== examples
"hello world"
"lo"
> "he wrd"

"abc"
""
> "abc"

"aaaa"
"a"
> ""

=== tests
""
"abc"
> ""

"The C Programming Language"
"aeiou"
> "Th C Prgrmmng Lngg"

"a.b.c"
"."
> "abc"

@@ category: C · Bits

@@@ problem c9 count-set-bits
title: Count Set Bits
difficulty: Easy
topics: Bit Manipulation
params: x
time: O(number of set bits)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Implement `int count_set_bits(uint32_t x)`, returning the number of `1` bits in `x`. Do not use `__builtin_popcount`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= x <= 2^32 - 1`

=== hints
- `x & (x - 1)` clears the lowest set bit (Kernighan's trick).

=== template
/* Number of 1 bits in x. */
int count_set_bits(uint32_t x) {
    /* your code here */
    return 0;
}

=== driver
extern "C" int count_set_bits(uint32_t x);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        uint32_t x = c.arg<uint32_t>();
        int r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = count_set_bits(x); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) { return lc::lines((uint32_t)g.range(0, 4294967295LL)); });
}

=== approach
**Kernighan's trick**: `x & (x - 1)` removes the lowest set bit, because subtracting 1 flips that bit and all the zeros below it. Loop until `x` is 0; the number of iterations equals the number of set bits.

=== solution
int count_set_bits(uint32_t x) {
    int count = 0;
    while (x) {
        x &= x - 1;     /* clear the lowest set bit */
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}

=== examples
11
> 3
: 11 = 0b1011.

0
> 0

4294967295
> 32

=== tests
1
> 1

2147483648
> 1

2863311530
> 16

@@@ problem c10 power-of-two
title: Power of Two
difficulty: Easy
topics: Bit Manipulation
params: x
time: O(1)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Implement `bool is_power_of_two(uint64_t x)`: return `true` if `x` is `2^k` for some `k >= 0`. Use a constant number of operations: no loops.

=== constraints
- `0 <= x <= 2^64 - 1`

=== hints
- A power of two has exactly one set bit.
- `x & (x - 1)` removes the lowest set bit. What is left for a power of two? Careful with `x = 0`.

=== template
/* true iff x is a power of two. */
bool is_power_of_two(uint64_t x) {
    /* your code here */
    return false;
}

=== driver
extern "C" bool is_power_of_two(uint64_t x);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        uint64_t x = c.arg<uint64_t>();
        bool r = false;
        c.time([&] { r = is_power_of_two(x); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) {
        uint64_t x = 1ULL << g.irange(0, 63);
        int k = g.irange(0, 3);
        if (k == 1) x += g.coin() ? 1 : -1;
        if (k == 2) x = g.rng();
        if (k == 3 && g.coin(0.2)) x = 0;
        return lc::lines(x);
    });
}

=== approach
A power of two has **exactly one** set bit. `x & (x - 1)` clears the lowest set bit, so it is zero exactly when `x` had at most one set bit. Excluding `x == 0` (no set bits) leaves the powers of two.

=== solution
bool is_power_of_two(uint64_t x) {
    return x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0;   /* exactly one bit set */
}

