@@@ problem c11 swap-endianness
title: Swap Byte Order (Endianness)
difficulty: Easy
topics: Bit Manipulation, Systems
params: x
time: O(1)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Implement `uint32_t swap_endian(uint32_t x)`, which reverses the order of the **four bytes** of `x` (e.g. to convert between little-endian and big-endian). `0x11223344` becomes `0x44332211`.

Values are written in decimal in the tests. Do not use `__builtin_bswap32` or `htonl`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= x <= 2^32 - 1`

=== hints
- Extract each byte with a shift and the mask `0xFF`, then shift it to its mirrored position.

=== template
/* Reverse the byte order of x. */
uint32_t swap_endian(uint32_t x) {
    /* your code here */
    return 0;
}

=== driver
extern "C" uint32_t swap_endian(uint32_t x);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        uint32_t x = c.arg<uint32_t>();
        uint32_t r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = swap_endian(x); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) { return lc::lines((uint32_t)g.range(0, 4294967295LL)); });
}

=== approach
Byte `i` (bits `8i .. 8i+7`) must move to byte `3 - i`. Isolate each byte with a mask and shift it: the lowest byte moves up 24 bits, the second up 8, the third down 8, and the top byte down 24. OR the four pieces together. Using `uint32_t` keeps all shifts well defined.

=== solution
uint32_t swap_endian(uint32_t x) {
    return ((x & 0x000000FFu) << 24) |   /* byte 0 -> byte 3 */
           ((x & 0x0000FF00u) << 8)  |   /* byte 1 -> byte 2 */
           ((x & 0x00FF0000u) >> 8)  |   /* byte 2 -> byte 1 */
           ((x & 0xFF000000u) >> 24);    /* byte 3 -> byte 0 */
}

=== examples
287454020
> 1144201745
: 0x11223344 becomes 0x44332211.

1
> 16777216
: 0x00000001 becomes 0x01000000.

=== tests
0
> 0

4294967295
> 4294967295

4278190080
> 255

@@@ problem c12 insert-bit-field
title: Insert a Bit Field
difficulty: Medium
topics: Bit Manipulation, Masks
params: x, pos, len, value
time: O(1)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Implement `uint32_t set_bits(uint32_t x, int pos, int len, uint32_t value)`: replace the `len` bits of `x` starting at bit `pos` (bit 0 = least significant) with the **low `len` bits** of `value`, and return the result. All other bits of `x` stay unchanged.

This is how hardware registers and packed bit fields are updated in C.

=== constraints
- `0 <= pos <= 31`, `1 <= len <= 32`, `pos + len <= 32`
- `value` may have bits above `len` set: ignore them

=== hints
- Build a mask of `len` ones: careful, `1u << 32` is undefined behaviour in C.
- Clear the field with `x & ~(mask << pos)`, then OR in `(value & mask) << pos`.

=== template
/* Replace bits [pos, pos+len) of x with the low len bits of value. */
uint32_t set_bits(uint32_t x, int pos, int len, uint32_t value) {
    /* your code here */
    return x;
}

=== driver
extern "C" uint32_t set_bits(uint32_t x, int pos, int len, uint32_t value);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        uint32_t x = c.arg<uint32_t>();
        int pos = c.arg<int>(), len = c.arg<int>();
        uint32_t v = c.arg<uint32_t>();
        if (pos < 0 || len < 1 || pos + len > 32) throw lc::InputError("need 0 <= pos, 1 <= len, pos + len <= 32");
        uint32_t r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = set_bits(x, pos, len, v); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) {
        int pos = g.irange(0, 31), len = g.irange(1, 32 - pos);
        if (g.coin(0.1)) pos = 0, len = 32;
        return lc::lines((uint32_t)g.range(0, 4294967295LL), pos, len, (uint32_t)g.range(0, 4294967295LL));
    });
}

=== approach
Make a mask of `len` one-bits. Since shifting a 32-bit value by 32 is undefined, handle `len == 32` separately (the mask is all ones), otherwise use `(1u << len) - 1`. Then:

1. clear the field in `x`: `x & ~(mask << pos)`;
2. place the new bits: `(value & mask) << pos` (masking first drops extra high bits of `value`);
3. OR the two together.

=== solution
uint32_t set_bits(uint32_t x, int pos, int len, uint32_t value) {
    uint32_t mask = (len >= 32) ? 0xFFFFFFFFu : ((1u << len) - 1u);   /* len ones; avoid 1u << 32 */
    x &= ~(mask << pos);                     /* clear the field */
    x |= (value & mask) << pos;              /* write the new bits */
    return x;
}

