@@@ problem c13 implement-memmove
title: Implement memmove
difficulty: Medium
topics: Pointers, Memory, Overlapping Buffers
params: buffer, dst, src, n
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=bytes_copied

=== description
Implement `void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)`: copy `n` bytes from `src` to `dst` and return `dst`. Unlike `memcpy`, the two regions **may overlap**, and the result must be as if the bytes were first copied to a temporary buffer.

Do not call `memmove`, `memcpy` or allocate memory. The test gives a buffer and byte offsets: the judge calls `my_memmove(buf + dst, buf + src, n)` and prints the buffer afterwards.

=== constraints
- `0 <= n`, and both ranges lie inside the buffer
- `0 <= buffer length <= 10^6`

=== hints
- If `dst` is before `src`, copying front-to-back is safe even when they overlap.
- If `dst` is after `src` and they overlap, front-to-back would overwrite source bytes before reading them: copy back-to-front.

=== template
/* Copy n bytes from src to dst; the regions may overlap. Return dst. */
void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n) {
    /* your code here */
    return dst;
}

=== driver
extern "C" void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>();
        long long d = c.arg<long long>(), sr = c.arg<long long>(), n = c.arg<long long>();
        if (d < 0 || sr < 0 || n < 0 || d + n > (long long)s.size() || sr + n > (long long)s.size())
            throw lc::InputError("ranges must lie inside the buffer");
        std::vector<char> buf(s.begin(), s.end());
        buf.push_back('\0');
        void *r = nullptr;
        c.time([&] { r = my_memmove(buf.data() + d, buf.data() + sr, (size_t)n); });
        if (r != buf.data() + d) { c.out_text("\"must return dst\""); return; }
        c.out(std::string(buf.begin(), buf.end() - 1));
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int len = std::max(n, 1);
        std::string s = g.str(len, "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789");
        if (n > 40) {   // benchmark: large overlapping copy in the tricky direction
            int cnt = len - len / 8;
            return lc::lines(s, (long long)(len - cnt), 0LL, (long long)cnt);
        }
        int cnt = g.irange(0, len);
        int d = g.irange(0, len - cnt), sr = g.irange(0, len - cnt);
        return lc::lines(s, (long long)d, (long long)sr, (long long)cnt);
    });
}

=== approach
Treat both pointers as `unsigned char *` and pick the copy direction:

- `dst < src` (or no overlap): copy **forward**. Each source byte is read before any write can reach it;
- `dst > src`: copy **backward**, from the last byte to the first, so the overlapping tail of the source is read before it is overwritten.

Comparing pointers into the same array is well defined, which is exactly the situation `memmove` is for.

=== solution
void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n) {
    unsigned char *d = (unsigned char *)dst;
    const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char *)src;
    if (d == s || n == 0) return dst;
    if (d < s) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) d[i] = s[i];          /* forward: safe when dst is before src */
    } else {
        for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--) d[i - 1] = s[i - 1];  /* backward: safe when dst is after src */
    }
    return dst;
}

=== examples
