@@@ problem c17 binary-search
title: Binary Search
difficulty: Easy
topics: Arrays, Binary Search, Pointers
params: arr, target
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1 unit=n

=== description
Implement `int binary_search(const int *arr, int n, int target)`: `arr` holds `n` **distinct** integers in ascending order. Return the index of `target`, or `-1` if it is absent.

In the tests the array is given as a list; the judge passes a pointer to its first element and its length.

=== constraints
- `0 <= n <= 10^6`
- values fit in `int`

=== hints
- Keep a range `[lo, hi]` that must contain the target if it exists.
- Compute the midpoint as `lo + (hi - lo) / 2` to avoid overflow.

=== template
/* Index of target in the sorted array arr[0..n-1], or -1. */
int binary_search(const int *arr, int n, int target) {
    /* your code here */
    return -1;
}

=== driver
extern "C" int binary_search(const int *arr, int n, int target);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        auto v = c.arg<std::vector<int>>();
        int t = c.arg<int>();
        int r = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = binary_search(v.data(), (int)v.size(), t); });
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        auto v = g.distinct(n, -3LL * n - 10, 3LL * n + 10);
        std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
        int t = (!v.empty() && g.coin()) ? g.pick(v) : g.irange(-3 * n - 12, 3 * n + 12);
        return lc::lines(v, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Maintain the closed range `[lo, hi]` in which the target could be. Look at the middle element: if it equals the target, done; if it is smaller, the target can only be to its right (`lo = mid + 1`); otherwise to its left (`hi = mid - 1`). The range halves each step, so at most about `log2(n) + 1` comparisons are made.

=== solution
int binary_search(const int *arr, int n, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;       /* no overflow, unlike (lo + hi) / 2 */
        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

=== examples
