@@@ problem c19 count-primes-sieve
title: Count Primes (Sieve of Eratosthenes)
difficulty: Medium
topics: Math, Arrays, Memory Management
params: n
time: O(n log log n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=1024..16777216 time=n space=n unit=n

=== description
Implement `int count_primes(int n)`, returning the number of prime numbers **strictly less than** `n`.

Use the **Sieve of Eratosthenes** with a heap-allocated array, and free it before returning (the judge reports leaks).

=== constraints
- `0 <= n <= 5 * 10^7`

=== hints
- Mark multiples of each prime `p` as composite, starting at `p * p` (smaller multiples were already marked).
- You only need primes `p` with `p * p < n` for marking.
- A `char` (or bit) per number keeps the memory small.

=== template
/* Number of primes p with p < n. */
int count_primes(int n) {
    /* your code here */
    return 0;
}

=== driver
extern "C" int count_primes(int n);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        int n = c.arg<int>();
        int r = 0;
        long long left = 0;
        c.time([&] { r = count_primes(n); left = lc::mem::cur; });
#ifndef LC_NO_TRACK
        if (left > 0) { c.out_text("\"memory leak: " + std::to_string(left) + " bytes not freed\""); return; }
#endif
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(n > 40 ? n : g.irange(0, 200)); });
}

=== approach
Allocate a byte array `composite[0..n-1]` initialized to zero. For each `p` from 2 while `p * p < n`: if `p` is not marked, it is prime, so mark `p*p, p*p + p, p*p + 2p, ...` as composite (smaller multiples have a smaller prime factor and are already marked). Finally count the unmarked numbers from 2 to `n - 1` and free the array.

The total marking work is `n · (1/2 + 1/3 + 1/5 + ...)` = O(n log log n), practically linear.

=== solution
int count_primes(int n) {
    if (n < 3) return 0;                         /* no primes below 2 */
    char *composite = calloc((size_t)n, 1);      /* zeroed: everything "maybe prime" */
    if (composite == NULL) return -1;
    for (long long p = 2; p * p < n; p++) {
        if (composite[p]) continue;              /* not a prime */
        for (long long m = p * p; m < n; m += p) composite[m] = 1;
    }
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 2; i < n; i++)
        if (!composite[i]) count++;
    free(composite);                             /* no leaks */
    return count;
}

