> 78498

@@@ problem c20 matrix-multiplication
title: Matrix Multiplication
difficulty: Medium
topics: Arrays, Pointers, Row-major Layout
params: A, B
time: O(n³)
space: O(1)
bench: n=8..256 time=n^3 space=1 unit=matrix_side_n

=== description
Implement `void mat_mul(const int *A, const int *B, long long *C, int n)`: multiply two `n x n` matrices stored in **row-major** order (element `(i, j)` is at index `i * n + j`) and write the product into `C`, which the caller has allocated.

Use `long long` accumulation: products of two `int`s can overflow an `int`.

Bonus: the loop order `i, k, j` walks memory sequentially and is much faster than `i, j, k` for large `n`.

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 256`
- `-10^4 <= A[i], B[i] <= 10^4`

=== hints
- `C[i][j] = sum over k of A[i][k] * B[k][j]`.
- With row-major storage, `A[i][k]` is `A[i * n + k]`.

=== template
/* C = A * B for n x n row-major matrices. C is allocated by the caller. */
void mat_mul(const int *A, const int *B, long long *C, int n) {
    /* your code here */
}

=== driver
extern "C" void mat_mul(const int *A, const int *B, long long *C, int n);
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        auto a = c.arg<std::vector<std::vector<int>>>(), b = c.arg<std::vector<std::vector<int>>>();
        int n = a.size();
        if ((int)b.size() != n) throw lc::InputError("A and B must be square matrices of the same size");
        std::vector<int> A, B;
        for (auto &r : a) { if ((int)r.size() != n) throw lc::InputError("A must be square"); A.insert(A.end(), r.begin(), r.end()); }
        for (auto &r : b) { if ((int)r.size() != n) throw lc::InputError("B must be square"); B.insert(B.end(), r.begin(), r.end()); }
        std::vector<long long> C((size_t)n * n, 7777777);   // garbage: C must be fully written
        c.time([&] { mat_mul(A.data(), B.data(), C.data(), n); });
        std::vector<std::vector<long long>> out(n, std::vector<long long>(n));
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) out[i][j] = C[(size_t)i * n + j];
        c.out(out);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(1, n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, 5) : n);
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> a(n), b(n);
        for (auto &r : a) r = g.ints(n, -10000, 10000);
        for (auto &r : b) r = g.ints(n, -10000, 10000);
        return lc::lines(a, b);
    });
}

=== approach
First set every `C[i][j]` to 0 (the caller's buffer may contain garbage). Then accumulate `C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j]` using the **`i, k, j`** loop order: for fixed `i` and `k`, the inner loop walks row `k` of `B` and row `i` of `C` sequentially in memory, which is cache friendly, while `A[i][k]` stays in a register. Casting to `long long` before multiplying avoids `int` overflow.

=== solution
void mat_mul(const int *A, const int *B, long long *C, int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n * n; i++) C[i] = 0;           /* output may contain garbage */
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
            long long a = A[i * n + k];                 /* reused across the whole row */
            const int *brow = &B[k * n];
            long long *crow = &C[i * n];
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) crow[j] += a * brow[j];   /* sequential access */
        }
    }
}

